## 7. Do-not-claim list

The Strategist should not, in the final draft, assert any of the following
as demonstrated facts:

1. "Silent-airport policies reduce missed boardings" — no published
   measurement supports this.
2. "LCY, HEL, or AMS practice transfers directly to IAD or DCA" — the run
   prompt explicitly forbids this and the evidence does not support it.
3. "The MWAA Design Manual currently lacks acoustic performance criteria"
   — the public record is silent, not proven absent.
4. "SFO achieved a 40% reduction in on-site noise" — cite the traceable
   47% / 60% paging-count and duration cuts (E-0032, E-0054) instead, and
   flag the 40% / 90-minute figures as self-reported.
5. "ACRP Report 175 recommends RT60 < 3.5 s, STI > 0.45, NC-55" as if
   from ACRP 175 itself — that combination is from a vendor-adjacent
   practitioner article. Cite ACRP's actual numbers (STI target 0.50,
   0.45 minimum, SNR ≥ 10 dB(A)) or NFPA 72's floor.
6. "A quiet-by-design standard will produce X% dwell-time or Y% concession
   spend uplift" — the ledger's dwell-and-spend claims are supported by
   peer trade press but no audited measurement.
7. "The 1:10:100 rule is a proven multiplier for airport terminal PA and
   acoustics" — it is a heuristic; use the direction, not the exact
   ratio. Use the Quant Analyst's 2×–4× construction benchmark instead.
8. "The July 2026 $22.5B Dulles announcement is a committed program" —
   it is not signed; trade press has flagged the missing budget-by-
   project, gate-count, and schedule detail.
9. "Announcements at silent airports have been eliminated" — every peer
   operator retains gate, disruption, and emergency announcements.
10. "MWAA controls the airline gate PA" — it does not; the 2025 Use &
    Lease grants airlines broadcast rights inside their leased envelope.
11. "MWAA's MII thresholds are X%" — the 2025 Use & Lease MII language is
    not verified in primary form; any specific number is inference.
12. "The AviaVox 20% boarding-efficiency figure" or "the $2.8M remediation
    case study" — vendor marketing and an unnamed-source anecdote
    respectively; not citable.
13. "The Denver-Southwest veto is a general pattern" — a single-source
    claim (E-0121); use as illustrative risk, not established fact.
14. "The NTSB assigned partial cause to DCA terminal communications" — it
    did not; the probable cause is FAA airspace design. Do not attach
    terminal-side communications to the NTSB findings.

